(11 + 3 in the meld). Wait, no. The meld is part of the hand. Wait, no, the meld is already a completed meld, so the remaining 11 tiles need to form the rest. So if they have a meld (3 tiles), the remaining 11 need to form four melds and a pair, or a special pattern.\n\nWait, the standard winning hand is four melds and a pair. So with one meld already (the W6-7-8 Chow), they need three more melds and a pair.\n\nLooking at their remaining 11 tiles: W1, W3, T1, T2, T4, T6, T9, F1, F4, J1, J3.\n\nThey need to form three more melds and a pair. Let's see possible combinations.\n\nLooking for possible pairs first. They have multiple single tiles. For example, if they can form a pair in the remaining tiles, that would help.\n\nPossible pairs:\n\n- W1 and W3: No, they are different.\n- T1, T2, T4, T6, T9: All singles.\n- F1, F4, J1, J3: All singles.\n\nNo existing pairs. So they need to form a pair from the remaining tiles. That might be difficult unless they can get a duplicate.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a Seven Pairs hand. For that, they need seven pairs. But with 11 tiles, they can't form seven pairs unless they have some duplicates. But they don't have any duplicates in their current hand. So that's not possible.\n\nAnother possibility is to form a special hand like All Honors or All Terminals, but they have a mix.\n\nLooking at the hand again, they have a mix of suits and honors. Maybe they can form a Half Flush (all in one suit and honors). But they have W (Characters), T (Bamboos), and J/F (Honors). Not all in one suit.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a Mixed Straight. For that, they need three chows in different suits. They already have a W6-8 Chow. If they can form two more chows in different suits.\n\nBut their current hand has T1, T2, T4, T6, T9. Maybe if they can get T3, they can form T1-2-3. Similarly, if they get T5, they can form T4-5-6. But they don't have those.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can discard a tile that's not useful and wait for