 shows that the previous player played T3, across played T4, and next played T9. So maybe T6 is less likely to be part of a Chow.\n\nAlternatively, playing T7. If someone has T6 and T8, they could Chi T7. But the previous player played T3, across played T4, next played T9. Not sure. Maybe T7 is a safer play.\n\nBut I'm not sure. Maybe the best option is to play T2, as it's a middle tile and more likely to be part of a Chi. So I'll choose to play T2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a pair of F1s and a meld of W6-W7-W8. Remaining tiles include W1, T1, T2, T4, T6, T7, B4, F4, J1. Playing T2 could allow opponents to form a Chi if they have T1/T3 or T3/T4, but it's a middle tile with more copies left. Keeping F1s for potential Pung is strategic.  \nReason: Playing T2 is a safe discard, reducing opponents' chances to form melds while preserving key tiles like F1s for future Pung or pair.  \nAnswer: Play T2"}
