1, so if I discard W1, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But if I keep it, perhaps I can form a pair later. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for possible pairs in the unshown tiles. For instance, J1 has 3 left. If I can get another J1, that would make a pair. Similarly, F4 has 2 left. If I can get another F4. \\n\\nBut I need to decide which tile to discard now. The valid actions are Play T2, B6, W8, J1, F4, W4, T7, W1. \\n\\nLet me evaluate each option. \\n\\nDiscarding T2: If I play T2, maybe someone can take it for a chow. For example, if someone has T1 and T3, they could chi T2. But I don't know. T2 is in my hand. If I discard it, it's out. \\n\\nDiscarding B6: Similar situation. B6 is a single. If someone has B5 or B7, they might take it. \\n\\nDiscarding W8: The existing meld is W6, W7, W8. If I discard W8, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But since it's part of the existing meld, maybe not. Wait, no, the existing meld is already there. The W8 in my hand is a separate tile. If I discard it, someone else could chi it if they have W6 and W7, but they might already have the meld. \\n\\nDiscarding J1: J1 is a single. If I discard it, maybe someone is going for a pung of J1. \\n\\nDiscarding F4: Similar to J1. \\n\\nDiscarding W4: W4 is a single. \\n\\nDiscarding T7: T7 is a single. \\n\\nDiscarding W1: W1 is a single. \\n\\nHmm. Since I need a pair, maybe I should discard a tile that is less likely to form a pair. For example, if I discard J1, and there are still 3 J1s left, maybe I can draw one later. Alternatively, if I discard W1, which has 2 left, maybe I can get another one. \\n\\nBut also, I need to consider the scoring patterns. Let's see. My current melds are two chows. The rest of the tiles are all singles. Maybe I can form another chow or pung. \\n\\nLooking at the tiles in hand: W1, W4, W8, T2, T7, B6, F4, J1. \\n\\nIs there a way to form another chow? For example, if I have W1, W2, W3. But I don't have W2 or W3. Similarly for others. \\n\